WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:04.060
Let's talk about the economy, starting with
jobs.

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:07.710
We had very, very strong jobs.

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:09.540
Numbers come out late last week.

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:18.140
CNBC reports job growth totals 253,000 in
April, beating expectations even as the U.S.

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:19.330
economy slows.

00:00:19.330 --> 00:00:24.330
From the standpoint of GDP, the simple facts
are nonfarm.

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:29.080
Payrolls were expected at 180,000 came in
at two 53,000.

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:30.720
That's great.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:32.950
Unemployment rate was 3.4%.

00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:34.800
The estimate was 3.6%.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:41.059
This is the lowest unemployment rate since
1969 and average hourly earnings went up half

00:00:41.059 --> 00:00:46.170
of 1% and are now up 4.4% from a year ago.

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:52.230
Wages growing faster than expected, both in
the last month and in the last year.

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:57.699
A couple of other interesting things to mention
here.

00:00:57.699 --> 00:01:03.079
One is that black unemployment, much touted
and discussed by Donald Trump as one of his

00:01:03.079 --> 00:01:06.480
major accomplishments, has hit an all time
low.

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:10.380
Do we care specifically about the racial breakdown
of jobs?

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:15.610
Well, we care to the extent that elected officials
in Washington use it as a political football.

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:21.049
Now, what does this say in general about how
the economy is doing?

00:01:21.049 --> 00:01:25.300
Well, we have to look at a lot of different
things when we ask how is an economy doing?

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:27.020
An economy is not only jobs.

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:33.159
An economy is not only stock market, an economy
is not only GDP or inflation, but in sum,

00:01:33.159 --> 00:01:34.159
total.

00:01:34.159 --> 00:01:35.470
We can look at a lot of these things.

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:40.470
For example, here is a chart of the Dow Jones
Industrial Average.

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:41.939
This is a one month chart.

00:01:41.939 --> 00:01:45.500
As you can see, the Dow is up over the last
month.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:47.100
Here is a six month chart.

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:50.149
The Dow is up over the last six months.

00:01:50.149 --> 00:01:51.689
Here is a year to date chart.

00:01:51.689 --> 00:01:53.189
The Dow is up there.

00:01:53.189 --> 00:01:54.540
Here is a one year chart.

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:56.289
The Dow is up there as well.

00:01:56.289 --> 00:01:58.090
And here is a five year chart.

00:01:58.090 --> 00:02:02.719
And the stock markets over longer periods
tend to go up and it is up then as well.

00:02:02.719 --> 00:02:08.729
So jobs numbers, quite solid, stock market,
quite solid inflation.

00:02:08.729 --> 00:02:13.750
Inflation now for the better part of a year
has been trickling down and down and down

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:18.750
with some blips where it goes up, our inflation
numbers where we would love them to be.

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:19.750
No.

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:24.489
Are they dramatically better than when Trump
left and Joe Biden came in?

00:02:24.489 --> 00:02:25.530
Absolutely.

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:27.069
And then we have GDP.

00:02:27.069 --> 00:02:32.769
GDP growth was still positive in Q1, but it
did slow down.

00:02:32.769 --> 00:02:38.959
Is this the most exciting, dynamic and explosive,
electrifying economy that we have ever seen?

00:02:38.959 --> 00:02:40.300
It is not.

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:46.510
Is this the near disaster that the right claims
the United States economy finds itself in

00:02:46.510 --> 00:02:48.980
because of Joe Biden's presence in the White
House?

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:50.060
Absolutely not.

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:55.459
And as I've said many times before, many of
these indicators only have little bits to

00:02:55.459 --> 00:03:00.700
do with the policy from the Oval Office and
the presidency.

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:05.560
Now, there are always reflexive people like
they can't help.

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:11.629
They just like vomit out these talking points
when they don't like conceding that the economy

00:03:11.629 --> 00:03:15.980
is doing okay because the person they didn't
vote for is in the Oval Office.

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:20.940
You will hear things like there's jobs created
because everybody's working two jobs and that

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:22.690
makes the economy bad.

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:24.269
We have an indicator of that.

00:03:24.269 --> 00:03:29.300
There are not a uniquely large number of people
working two jobs at this point in time.

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:33.900
You hear about, well, the reason it's happening
is because stay at home moms are having to

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:37.189
go back to work because the economy is so
bad.

00:03:37.189 --> 00:03:42.430
Yet you never want to talk about that dynamic
when you do your labor participation rate

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:43.510
conspiracy theory.

00:03:43.510 --> 00:03:49.150
So we we hear about all of these things year
in and year out.

00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:50.970
We can find the data.

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:56.829
There is not anything particularly alarming
or different right now about the number of

00:03:56.829 --> 00:04:00.720
people who are out of the workforce and went
back in or the number of people.

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:01.970
Or working two jobs.

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:03.849
It doesn't mean we don't have problems.

00:04:03.849 --> 00:04:07.049
Wages relative to inflation have been stagnant
for a while now.

00:04:07.049 --> 00:04:08.500
That's a long term problem.

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:09.500
It's bigger than Biden.

00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:10.500
It's bigger than Trump.

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:11.500
It's bigger than Obama.

00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:13.000
Okay, you get the picture.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:14.489
Labor participation rate.

00:04:14.489 --> 00:04:18.150
The country's population has been growing
for a very long time.

00:04:18.150 --> 00:04:23.699
At most times you can say we have more people
than ever working and we have more people

00:04:23.699 --> 00:04:25.070
than ever not working.

00:04:25.070 --> 00:04:28.880
That makes it a really popular political football
to throw around.

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:32.880
My suggestion is to zoom out.

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:35.009
Look at unemployment.

00:04:35.009 --> 00:04:36.190
Job creation.

00:04:36.190 --> 00:04:37.300
GDP.

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:38.410
Stocks.

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:39.510
Inflation.

00:04:39.510 --> 00:04:41.760
Meaning Consumer Price index.

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:42.760
Wage growth.

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:48.360
And I could go on and a couple more and then
take the general temperature of it to get

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:49.949
a sense of where we are.

00:04:49.949 --> 00:04:55.820
If you do that, it is not possible to come
to the conclusion some on the right have come

00:04:55.820 --> 00:05:01.230
to, which is that Joe Biden's economy is a
disaster, putting the United States on the

00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:02.850
brink of collapse.

00:05:02.850 --> 00:05:10.340
There is no case that can be made with data,
actual data, honest data that would accurately

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:12.430
suggest that that is the situation right now.

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:13.930
Could the economy be better?

00:05:13.930 --> 00:05:14.930
Sure.

00:05:14.930 --> 00:05:16.470
Is the economy a disaster right now?

00:05:16.470 --> 00:05:17.470
Not at all.

00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:18.470
Could it become one in the future?

00:05:18.470 --> 00:05:18.472
Of course.

